        "stdout": "# Project workflow\n\nUse Beads for issue work. At session start or after compaction, run\n`bd prime --no-memories` to restore the project workflow. Read the assigned issue\nwith `bd show <issue-id>` before implementing. Start it with\n`bd update <issue-id> --status in_progress`.\n\nUse the Beads skill in `.agents/skills/beads/SKILL.md` for this workflow. Implement\nthe issue's requirements, verify the resulting behavior, and close the issue\nwith `bd close <issue-id>` when its work is complete. Consult command help when\nsyntax is unclear. Preserve earlier files and task history.\n\n# Project memory\n\nMemories carry approved intent and verified findings into later issue work.\nPreserve policy scope, exceptions, rationale, and engineering constraints whose\nmeaning a later agent cannot establish from the implementation alone. A task log,\na list of changed files, and unsupported guesses do not serve that purpose.\n\n## Before a policy-dependent change\n\nRead the applicable retained knowledge before implementing behavior governed by\nan earlier agreement or investigation. A known relevant key from the project\ncatalog, issue history, or another record supports direct lookup:\n\n```text\nbd recall '<key>'\n```\n\nWithout a known relevant reference, search by project and topic, then read the\nfull applicable result:\n\n```text\nbd memories '<query>'\nbd recall '<key>'\n```\n\nSearch matches literal substrings in keys and bodies; shorten a query when needed.\nConfirm project, scope, version, and evidence before applying a record. If records\nare absent or incomplete, consult legitimate approval sources, docs, tests, and\nissue history. Resolve conflicts against those sources. Distinguish an approval\nfrom an inference and a remembered claim from evidence.\n\n## Before closing a knowledge-producing issue\n\nCapture newly approved durable decisions and verified reusable findings, including\nscope or rationale beyond the feature implemented today. First inspect existing\nknowledge on the topic to reuse a current key for the same agreement and avoid\nduplicates. Choose a descriptive project/topic key for new knowledge.\n\nGive the record a readable opening stating its purpose and applicability. Retain\nexact identifiers, types, units, boundaries, structure, and exceptions needed for\nlater work. Use structured data where the agreement is structured and faithful\nprose where prose fits. Cite actual approval or investigation sources. For a\nworkaround, include tool/version, circumstances, evidence, and when to recheck it;\na past failure is not a permanent avoidance policy. Report only validation that\nactually occurred.\n\n```text\nbd remember '<complete record>' --key '<key>'\nbd recall '<key>'\n```\n\nCheck the save acknowledgment and full readback for faithful retention. Readback\nverifies storage, not whether the saved claim is true. Include the record key in\nthe issue's completion context so later work can follow it.\n\n## Revision, history, and reproduction\n\nFor a permanent revision, read the actual current record and preserve any earlier\nknowledge needed for historical work under a descriptive historical key before\nupdating the current key. Carry unaffected facts forward. If no earlier record\nexists, recover what the approval sources support and describe its source\naccurately; never invent an earlier capture or verification.\n\nHistorical work uses the applicable old agreement while leaving the current one\nand unrelated historical notes intact. Implementing another consumer of an\nexisting agreement does not create a new agreement. Fully supplied one-off\nreproduction ordinarily requires neither retrieval nor another capture. Save\nonly if the work actually establishes new durable knowledge or changes it.\n\nThese legacy keys identify mutable notes. Historical keys are naming conventions,\nnot immutable revision pins or protection against concurrent writers.\n\n## Project reference catalog\n\nAt session start, if BEADS_MEMORY_INDEX is set, read the JSON file it names for\nthe available memory keys. This catalog is a reference index, not the contents\nof those records. Use the project memory procedure to decide which records the\nwork needs. An unset variable means this project uses search without a catalog.\n",
